Source package: gnome-text-editor

requests-wait-for-session-restore.patch
    Text Editor loads its session asynchronously when it starts, and the first activation or the first files to open
    start the restore. An activation which arrived before the session was loaded, such as a second launch from the
    dock while Text Editor started, found no window and restored the session a second time, which opened each window
    of the session and each of its documents twice, two of them saving to the same draft. Files to open which arrived
    meanwhile opened before the session added its pages, so a file of the session was open twice.

    editor_application_activate and editor_application_open now queue the requests which arrive while the session is
    restored, and editor_application_restore_cb handles them once it is restored, as if they arrived after it. With
    restore-window-placement.patch, the windows GTK restored wait for their pages until then, and with
    launch-opens-new-window.patch, each of the requests opens a window of its own.

    The patch applies to Text Editor's main branch as it does to 50.1, and is not yet proposed upstream. It is tested
    by the third run of tests/gnome-text-editor/run-text-editor-session.sh, which holds the session file back until
    two more launches arrived. Drop it once Text Editor handles the requests which arrive while it restores its
    session.

restore-window-placement.patch
    Lets Text Editor's windows return to their workspace, monitor, position and size when it restores its session,
    with the session save/restore API of GtkApplication, which only the GTK of patches/gtk4/ has. Text Editor keeps
    restoring the documents of its windows from its own session state, ~/.local/share/org.gnome.TextEditor/
    session.gvariant, and GTK saves and restores the names under which mutter remembers where each window was.

    Every window has an ID, saved as window-id both with the window in session.gvariant and in GTK's window state
    of it, which links the two. When Text Editor starts, GTK emits GtkApplication::restore-window for each window
    it saved, and Text Editor creates an empty window for it, kept hidden, which GTK gives the name mutter knows it
    by. The session restore then gives that window the documents saved with the same ID and shows it. A window GTK
    restored which the session restore has no documents for, for example when Restore Session is switched off, is
    destroyed again once the session has another window. A window in session.gvariant without an ID GTK restored,
    such as every window at the first start with the patch, opens unplaced and keeps its ID, so that it is placed
    from the next start on.

    The property and the signals of the save/restore API are looked up at runtime, so the package builds against
    Ubuntu's GTK, whose headers do not have them, and behaves exactly as before when it runs with Ubuntu's GTK. A
    standalone instance (--standalone) does not set GtkApplication:support-save, since it runs next to the primary
    one and both would write the same state file.

    The pages of a window stay in session.gvariant rather than in GTK's window state because Text Editor saves and
    loads them asynchronously, along with its drafts, while GTK requires the window to exist before
    restore-window returns.

    Quitting with Ctrl+Q leaves the windows open until the process exits, and mutter keeps their places only with
    patches/mutter/save-toplevels-on-session-destroy.patch. Without it, only the first window Text Editor opened
    returns to its place.

    The patch adds no test to Text Editor's test suite, which has no tests of the session. It is tested together with
    patches/gtk4/ and patches/mutter/ by tests/gnome-text-editor/run-text-editor-session.sh, see
    tests/gnome-text-editor/README.

    Drop the patch once Text Editor uses a save/restore API which a GTK in Ubuntu has. After a version bump of
    Text Editor, check that editor_session_restore_v1 in src/editor-session.c still creates the windows it restores
    with _editor_session_create_window_no_draft, and that editor_application_activate in src/editor-application.c
    still restores the session only when no window is open.

hide-restored-banners.patch
    Text Editor shows a "Document Restored" banner above every restored document which was never saved to a file,
    and a "Draft Changes Restored" banner above every restored document with unsaved changes to a file. With
    restore-window-placement.patch, documents return with their unsaved content and their windows at every start,
    so a document kept unsaved is expected, and neither banner is shown any more. The window title still shows
    whether a document is unsaved or modified. The patch removes the branch for restored documents from
    editor_info_bar_update in src/editor-info-bar.c. Drop it once Text Editor no longer shows these banners.

schedule-auto-save-after-early-changes.patch, reschedule-auto-save-on-delay-change.patch, run-saves-one-at-a-time.patch
    Text Editor autosaves its session, the drafts of unsaved documents and session.gvariant, auto-save-delay seconds
    (3 by default) after a change, so that a crash or a power loss loses only the last seconds. Three bugs in
    src/editor-session.c undo that, and each patch fixes one of them. The patches are the three commits of
    https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/gnome-text-editor/-/merge_requests/261, written against Text Editor's main
    branch, which they apply to as they do to 50.1.

    schedule-auto-save-after-early-changes.patch: Text Editor schedules an auto-save only when a change marks a
    clean session dirty, and a change while auto-save is still disabled marks the session dirty without scheduling
    one, after which nothing is autosaved until Text Editor quits. With restore-window-placement.patch this happens
    at every start after the first, because Text Editor creates the windows GTK restores before it enables
    auto-save. Without the patch it happens with any auto-save-delay other than the default, which Text Editor
    applies first. The patch marks the session dirty only together with scheduling an auto-save.

    reschedule-auto-save-on-delay-change.patch: changing auto-save-delay while an auto-save is pending removes it,
    and marking the session dirty again schedules none, because the session is still dirty. The patch tracks a
    scheduled auto-save by its timeout source instead of a separate dirty flag.

    run-saves-one-at-a-time.patch: a save captures the session state when it is requested, and writes it after its
    drafts, which takes seconds for a large draft, so a later save could write its newer state first and then be
    overwritten with the older one. The patch runs saves one at a time, in the order they are requested, replaces
    a waiting auto-save by a newer one, and completes the saves still waiting when a save for shutdown finishes
    without running them, since Text Editor quits then.

    The patches add no test to Text Editor's test suite, which has no tests of the session. They are tested by
    tests/gnome-text-editor/run-text-editor-autosave.sh, see tests/gnome-text-editor/README, whose scenario
    windows-restored-by-gtk covers the start with restore-window-placement.patch. Drop each patch once Ubuntu's Text
    Editor includes the merge request. After a version bump, check that _editor_session_mark_dirty in src/editor-session.c is still the only
    place an auto-save is scheduled.

auto-save-on-state-changes.patch
    Text Editor's session state has more than the drafts and which windows and pages there are: the selection of
    every page, the size of every window, whether it is maximized, the order of its pages and which one is selected,
    and the language and the encoding of every page, all of which the session restore applies. Only changes of the
    text, of the drafts and of which windows and pages there are schedule an auto-save, though, so a crash restores the
    rest as it was at the last such change, usually the last edit. The patch marks the session dirty also from a
    document's cursor-moved signal and its language and encoding notifications, from the notifications of a window's
    default size and maximized state, and from its tab view's selected page and its page-reordered, page-attached and
    page-detached signals, which cover a page dragged in from another window. Typing moves the cursor, but schedules no
    further saves, because an auto-save already scheduled saves the cursor too, and a save after only such a change
    writes session.gvariant without drafts. GTK updates the default size of a window when it is resized while it is not
    maximized, fullscreen or tiled, which is the size the session state has.

    The session state marks the window which had the focus as well, but the session restore does not read it, so a
    change of focus schedules no auto-save.

    The patch is local only and not proposed upstream. It is tested by the scenarios cursor-moved, tab-selected,
    tab-moved, window-maximized and window-resized of tests/gnome-text-editor/run-text-editor-autosave.sh. The language
    and the encoding are set in dialogs, which the test does not drive. After a version bump, check that
    editor_session_add_page and editor_session_add_window in src/editor-session.c still exist and that the session
    state has no new parts which are changed without marking the session dirty.

launch-opens-new-window.patch
    Makes a start of Text Editor behave like a launch while it runs: the restored windows return to their places
    without being activated, and the launch, or files opened from outside, such as with xdg-open or from the
    terminal, open in a new window on the current workspace, which has the focus. Text Editor restored its session
    by presenting each restored window, and GTK gives the activation token of the launch to the first window it
    shows: with the token of a launch from the dock, mutter moved that restored window to the workspace of the
    launch and focused it, or, when a window received input or was activated after the launch, marked it as
    demanding attention. Files opened from outside became tabs of the most recently used window, wherever it was,
    and a launch opened no new window.

    Restored windows are now shown with GTK's automatic startup notification off, and a restored window whose files
    and drafts were all removed is discarded rather than given a new draft. The request which restored the session
    opens a window of its own afterwards: a new window with a draft for a launch, or one with the files. While Text
    Editor runs, activation and files opened from outside open a new window as well. A file which is open already
    is shown in its window, as before. Mutter focuses a window which maps without a token and shows several newly
    mapped windows in an order of its own, so the new window waits until mutter showed each restored window on a
    monitor, or 250 ms after the last of them drew its first frame, since one on another workspace is not shown,
    and at most a second. A window with only an unmodified draft is saved without pages and not restored, so a
    launch adds no window to the next start, but GTK saves its window state, which the next start discards.

    The patch is local only and not proposed upstream. It is tested by
    tests/gnome-text-editor/run-text-editor-session.sh, whose second run is launched with an activation token. After a
    version bump, check that editor_session_restore_v1 still shows its windows in one place and that
    editor_application_restore_cb still runs after every restore.

restored-documents-hold-no-logout.patch
    Keeps Text Editor from holding a logout for unsaved documents while it restores its session, since they return at
    its next start. Every window with an unsaved document inhibited the logout, so GNOME Shell's logout dialog listed
    Text Editor as having unsaved work. When the session ends, GTK quits Text Editor without the save for shutdown
    which closing the last window or Ctrl+Q runs first, so the next start restored the state of the last auto-save,
    as after a crash, without the text typed since.

    editor_window_update_inhibit now inhibits nothing while the session restores its pages, and the application's
    shutdown saves the session when no save for shutdown was requested before, and runs the main context until the
    saves finished, for at most 5 seconds. Without session restore, with restore-session off, --ignore-session or
    --standalone, unsaved documents still hold the logout and nothing is saved at shutdown, since the next start then
    restores the documents only from a state not marked as shut down, as after a crash.

    The patch is local only and not proposed upstream. It is tested by the session test text-editor in
    tests/session/, which types into a document right before logging out: Text Editor holds no inhibitor then, and
    the text returns after logging in again. After a version bump, check that GTK still quits the application with
    g_application_quit() when the session ends, in gtk/gtkapplication-dbus.c, and that editor_application_shutdown
    still runs before the session is disposed.

last-tab-closes-window.patch
    Closes a window once its last tab was closed, with Ctrl+W or with :q in Vim mode, instead of showing the empty
    state of the window, "Start or Open a Document", which a second Ctrl+W closed. A window whose last tab moved to
    another window, with Move to New Window (Ctrl+Shift+N), closes as well. The last window closing quits Text
    Editor, which saves the session as it does when the window is closed otherwise, and since its tab was closed
    before, the next start restores no window for it.

    editor_window_items_changed_cb closes a window when a page was removed and none is left. It closes it from an idle
    callback, which runs before the window is redrawn without pages, because the tab view is still removing the page,
    and because text read from standard input replaces an empty draft by removing the draft and adding its own page
    right after, which keeps the window open. Ctrl+Shift+T reopens closed tabs only in the window they were closed in,
    so the files of a window which closed are reopened from the recent documents of the open menu.

    Upstream keeps the empty state by design: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/gnome-text-editor/-/issues/404 was moved
    to https://gitlab.gnome.org/Teams/Design/whiteboards/-/issues/58, which is still open, and
    https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/gnome-text-editor/-/issues/681 was closed as working as designed. The patch is local
    only and not proposed upstream. It is tested by the scenarios last-tab-closes-window and standard-input-keeps-window
    of tests/gnome-text-editor/run-text-editor-autosave.sh. After a version bump, check that
    editor_window_items_changed_cb in src/editor-window.c is still connected to the items-changed signal of the pages
    of the tab view, and that editor_session_open_stream in src/editor-session.c still adds the page of standard input
    in the same main loop iteration in which it removes the draft.

last-window-leaves-session.patch
    Leaves a window you close out of the session, the last one too. Text Editor removes a window you close from its
    session with its documents, except the last one, which it saves with the session before it quits, so after closing
    every window one after the other, its next start restores the window closed last. With the patch, the window
    closed last leaves the session before that save, and the next start opens a new window with an empty document.
    Before a window with unsaved changes closes, Text Editor still asks whether to save or discard them.

    Every window still returns when Text Editor ends without you closing its windows. The quit action, Ctrl+Q, saves
    the session with every window before it quits, and so does the end of the desktop session, see
    restored-documents-hold-no-logout.patch, while SIGTERM, which Text Editor does not handle, and a crash leave the
    session autosaved last. _editor_session_remove_window receives whether you closed the window, which
    editor_window_do_close passes, while editor_window_dispose keeps a window destroyed otherwise in the session, as
    upstream does. The patch is local only, like last-tab-closes-window.patch. It is tested by run 5 of
    tests/gnome-text-editor/run-text-editor-session.sh, and by the scenario last-tab-closes-window of
    tests/gnome-text-editor/run-text-editor-autosave.sh, which has to quit with no window in the session. After a
    version bump, check that editor_window_do_close and editor_window_dispose in src/editor-window.c are still the only
    callers of _editor_session_remove_window.

Check by hand after an upgrade, in a new login, so that the patched mutter runs, and with no Text Editor of the
previous version still running in the background:

  open two windows with a document each on different workspaces, quit with Ctrl+Q, and start Text Editor again:
  both windows should return to their workspaces and places, and a new window with an empty document should open on
  the current workspace, with the focus. The first start after installing the patch has no names saved yet, so the
  windows are placed from the second start on.
  open a file from the terminal with gnome-text-editor or xdg-open while Text Editor runs: it should open in a new
  window on the current workspace.
  type into a new document and log out right away: the logout dialog should not list Text Editor, and after logging
  in again, Text Editor should return the document with all the text typed.
  with two windows open, press Ctrl+W on the last tab of one of them: that window should close and the other stay.
  Ctrl+W on the last tab of the other one should quit Text Editor, and its next start should open only a new window
  with an empty document. Type into a new document in a second window, close that window with its close button and
  choose Discard: the window should close, and journalctl --user -b should show no editor-window criticals.
  open two windows with a document each, close both with their close buttons, and start Text Editor: only a new
  window with an empty document should open. Open two windows again, quit with Ctrl+Q, and start Text Editor: both
  windows should return.
